---
<div class="container-custom" style="padding-bottom: 4rem;" id="guide">
  
  <!-- Comprehensive Technical Framing Guide -->
  <div class="geist-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <span class="mono-eyebrow">Comprehensive Roof Framing & Technical Guide</span>
    <h2 class="heading-lg" style="margin-top: 4px;">Mastering Roof Rafter Calculations: Complete Framing Guide</h2>
    
    <div class="body-lg" style="margin-top: 12px; color: var(--color-body); line-height: 1.7;">
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
        Constructing a durable, code-compliant roof requires precision at every step. Whether you are a professional framing contractor, builder, architect, or ambitious DIYer, <strong>figuring rafters</strong> accurately is the foundation of structural integrity. Our free <strong>Rafter Calculator</strong> simplifies complex trigonometry into instant, reliable measurements for common rafters, plumb cuts, seat cuts, eave overhangs, and lumber material estimates.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
        When <strong>figuring out rafter length</strong>, even a fraction of an inch error can lead to sagging rooflines, misaligned ridge boards, or uneven overhangs. By using an advanced <strong>Rafter Calculator</strong>, you eliminate guesswork, save valuable time on the job site, and ensure every board is cut to exact specifications before swinging a hammer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 1: How to Determine Rafter Length -->
  <div class="geist-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <span class="mono-eyebrow" style="color: var(--color-accent-deep);">Step-by-Step Framing Process</span>
    <h3 class="heading-md" style="margin-top: 4px;">How to Determine Rafter Length & Calculate Rafter Span</h3>
    
    <p class="body-md" style="margin-top: 8px; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      To <strong>determine rafter length</strong> correctly, you must understand the basic right-triangle geometry of roof framing. The three key variables in any <strong>rafter dimensions calculator</strong> are the horizontal run, the vertical rise, and the sloping hypotenuse representing the actual <strong>length of rafter</strong>.
    </p>

    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 20px;">
      <!-- Step 1 -->
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 1.25rem; background-color: var(--color-canvas-elevated);">
        <div style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 8px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="width: 26px; height: 26px; background: var(--color-ink); color: #fff; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 0.8125rem; font-family: var(--font-mono);">1</span>
          Calculate Effective Run
        </div>
        <p class="body-md" style="margin-bottom: 12px; color: var(--color-body);">
          When <strong>calculating rafter span</strong>, measure the total outside-to-outside building span. Because rafters meet against a center ridge beam, subtract half the actual ridge board thickness (e.g., 0.75" for a nominal 1.5" 2x ridge board) from half the total span.
        </p>
        <div class="code-block" style="background-color: var(--color-hairline-soft); padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: var(--radius-sm); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-ink); font-weight: 600;">
          Effective Run = (Total Span - Ridge Thickness) / 2
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 2 -->
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 1.25rem; background-color: var(--color-canvas-elevated);">
        <div style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 8px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="width: 26px; height: 26px; background: var(--color-ink); color: #fff; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 0.8125rem; font-family: var(--font-mono);">2</span>
          Apply Pythagorean Theorem
        </div>
        <p class="body-md" style="margin-bottom: 12px; color: var(--color-body);">
          To <strong>figure rafter length</strong> from the wall plate to the ridge, apply the Pythagorean formula. Multiply the effective run by the pitch ratio (Rise in 12) or the tangent of the pitch angle to find the total rise, then calculate the hypotenuse.
        </p>
        <div class="code-block" style="background-color: var(--color-hairline-soft); padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: var(--radius-sm); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
          Base Length = √(Effective Run² + Rise²)
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 3 -->
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 1.25rem; background-color: var(--color-canvas-elevated);">
        <div style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 8px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="width: 26px; height: 26px; background: var(--color-ink); color: #fff; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 0.8125rem; font-family: var(--font-mono);">3</span>
          Add Overhang & Eaves Extension
        </div>
        <p class="body-md" style="margin-bottom: 12px; color: var(--color-body);">
          To <strong>find rafter length</strong> total, calculate the diagonal length of the eave overhang extending past the exterior wall. Adding this overhang to the base rafter length gives the complete <strong>rafter calculator length</strong> required when buying lumber.
        </p>
        <div class="code-block" style="background-color: var(--color-hairline-soft); padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: var(--radius-sm); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-ink); font-weight: 600;">
          Total Length = Base Length + Overhang Hypotenuse
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 2: Birdsmouth Cuts & HAP Code Requirements -->
  <div class="geist-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 24px;" class="guide-split-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="mono-eyebrow" style="color: var(--color-warning-deep);">Structural Code Compliance</span>
        <h3 class="heading-md" style="margin-top: 4px;">Understanding Birdsmouth Cuts & Height Above Plate (HAP)</h3>
        <p class="body-md" style="margin-top: 8px; line-height: 1.6;">
          When <strong>figuring roof rafters</strong>, cutting the notch where the board rests on the wall plate requires careful attention. Known as a <strong>Birdsmouth Cut</strong>, this notch consists of a horizontal seat cut (resting flat on the wall top plate) and a vertical plumb cut.
        </p>
        <p class="body-md" style="margin-top: 8px; line-height: 1.6;">
          The remaining wood depth measured vertically directly above the outer corner of the wall plate is called the <strong>Height Above Plate (HAP)</strong>. Maintaining adequate HAP is crucial for bearing heavy roof loads, snow accumulation, and high winds.
        </p>
        
        <div style="background-color: var(--color-warning-soft); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-warning); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 0.875rem; color: #7c2d12; margin-top: 14px;">
          <strong>International Residential Code (IRC) Rule:</strong> Never cut a birdsmouth notch deeper than <strong>one-third (1/3)</strong> of the total rafter board depth! You must preserve at least <strong>two-thirds (2/3)</strong> of the rafter width for HAP to prevent structural shear failure.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- HAP Technical Spec Sheet -->
      <div style="background-color: #0f172a; border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 1.5rem; color: #fff; display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center;">
        <span class="mono-eyebrow" style="color: #38bdf8;">Birdsmouth Cut Rules</span>
        <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700; color: #ffffff; margin-top: 4px;">Framing Notch Guidelines</h4>
        
        <div style="margin-top: 16px; background-color: #1e293b; border: 1px solid #334155; padding: 14px; border-radius: 6px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem;">
          <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; padding: 6px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #334155;">
            <span style="color: #94a3b8;">Seat Cut Length:</span>
            <span style="color: #ffffff; font-weight: 600;">Width of Wall Plate (e.g. 3.5")</span>
          </div>
          <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; padding: 6px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #334155;">
            <span style="color: #94a3b8;">Plumb Notch Depth:</span>
            <span style="color: #f5a623; font-weight: 600;">Seat Width × tan(Pitch Angle)</span>
          </div>
          <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; padding: 6px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #334155;">
            <span style="color: #94a3b8;">Max Notch Depth:</span>
            <span style="color: #ef4444; font-weight: 600;">≤ 1/3 Rafter Board Depth</span>
          </div>
          <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; padding: 6px 0;">
            <span style="color: #94a3b8;">Minimum HAP:</span>
            <span style="color: #10b981; font-weight: 600;">≥ 2/3 Rafter Board Depth</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 3: Roof Pitch Chart & Multipliers -->
  <div class="geist-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <span class="mono-eyebrow">Quick Reference Matrix</span>
    <h3 class="heading-md" style="margin-top: 4px;">Roof Pitch Conversion Chart & Multipliers</h3>
    <p class="body-md" style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
      Whether you are using our <strong>rafter length calc</strong> or calculating manually, this table provides quick reference values for standard pitch ratios, slope angles in degrees, slope percentages, and rafter length multipliers.
    </p>

    <div style="overflow-x: auto;">
      <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 0.875rem;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="background-color: var(--color-hairline-soft); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-hairline);">
            <th style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Pitch Ratio (Rise:12)</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Pitch Angle (Deg)</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Slope Grade (%)</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Pitch Multiplier</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Common Roof Type</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody style="font-family: var(--font-mono);">
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);">
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-accent);">3:12</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">14.04°</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">25.0%</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">1.031</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-family: var(--font-sans); color: var(--color-body);">Shed & Porch Roofs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);">
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-accent);">4:12</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">18.43°</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">33.3%</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">1.054</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-family: var(--font-sans); color: var(--color-body);">Standard Low Pitch Ranch</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);">
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-accent);">5:12</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">22.62°</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">41.7%</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">1.083</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-family: var(--font-sans); color: var(--color-body);">Conventional Residential Roof</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline); background-color: var(--color-hairline-soft);">
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-accent);">6:12 (Standard)</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 700;">26.57°</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 700;">50.0%</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 700;">1.118</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-family: var(--font-sans); font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Most Popular Roof Pitch</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);">
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-accent);">8:12</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">33.69°</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">66.7%</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">1.202</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-family: var(--font-sans); color: var(--color-body);">Steep Pitch / Colonial Homes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);">
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-accent);">10:12</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">39.81°</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">83.3%</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">1.302</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-family: var(--font-sans); color: var(--color-body);">High Snow & Alpine Regions</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-accent);">12:12</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">45.00°</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">100.0%</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px;">1.414</td>
            <td style="padding: 10px 12px; font-family: var(--font-sans); color: var(--color-body);">A-Frame & Steep Gables</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 4: Site-Built Rafters vs. Engineered Roof Trusses -->
  <div class="geist-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <span class="mono-eyebrow">Framing System Comparison</span>
    <h3 class="heading-md" style="margin-top: 4px;">Site-Cut Rafters vs. Factory Roof Trusses</h3>
    
    <p class="body-md" style="margin-top: 8px; line-height: 1.6;">
      When planning a roofing project, builders often compare traditional stick-built common rafters with pre-engineered roof trusses. While a <strong>roof truss calculator</strong> is used for factory-assembled webbed truss units, our <strong>Rafter Calculator</strong> is designed specifically for custom, hand-cut framing constructed directly on-site.
    </p>

    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; margin-top: 1rem;">
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 1.25rem;">
        <h4 style="font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 6px;">Stick-Built Common Rafters</h4>
        <ul style="padding-left: 18px; color: var(--color-body); font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.6;">
          <li>Custom-cut on job site to fit any building span or unique angle.</li>
          <li>Creates usable attic storage, vaulted ceilings, or living space.</li>
          <li>Easier to transport lumber to remote building sites.</li>
          <li>Calculated using our <strong>Rafter calculator free</strong> online tool.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 1.25rem;">
        <h4 style="font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 6px;">Pre-Engineered Roof Trusses</h4>
        <ul style="padding-left: 18px; color: var(--color-body); font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.6;">
          <li>Pre-fabricated in factories using metal connector plates.</li>
          <li>Spans longer distances without interior load-bearing walls.</li>
          <li>Faster roof enclosure time on simple rectangular plans.</li>
          <li>Calculated via specialized <strong>blocklayer rafter calculator</strong> or truss engineering software.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 5: Accessible Semantic HTML FAQ Section -->
  <section class="geist-card" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
    <span class="mono-eyebrow">Help & Knowledge Base</span>
    <h2 class="heading-lg" id="faq-heading" style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Rafter Calculator FAQ</h2>

    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px;">

      <!-- FAQ Item 1 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">What is the Rafter Calculator?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>The Rafter Calculator is a free web-based construction tool designed to help carpenters, roofers, builders, and DIYers determine accurate rafter lengths, roof pitch angles, birdsmouth cut dimensions, and material requirements directly in their web browser.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 2 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">How do you calculate rafter length with a calculator?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>To calculate rafter length with a calculator, measure your roof's effective horizontal run and vertical rise, then apply the right-triangle Pythagorean formula: Rafter Length = √(Run² + Rise²). You then adjust the result for half the ridge board thickness and add any desired eave overhang.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 3 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">How do you work out rafter length using a Rafter Calculator?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Working out rafter length using our Rafter Calculator is simple: enter your total roof span or half run, select your roof pitch ratio or slope angle in degrees, and input your overhang distance. The tool automatically computes the exact rafter length, total rise, plumb cut angle, and seat cut width in real time.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 4 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">How does a Rafter Calculator work with a diagram?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Our Rafter Calculator features an interactive 2D vector schematic canvas that updates dynamically as you change your inputs. The visual diagram illustrates the angled rafter board, ridge beam intersection, wall plate, birdsmouth notch, and overhang with clear dimension callout lines, allowing you to visually verify all cuts before making your first saw mark.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 5 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">Is there a free Rafter Calculator app?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Yes, Raftercalculator.org is a completely free web application accessible on any desktop, tablet, or smartphone without registration, subscriptions, or app store installation required.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 6 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">Does the Rafter Calculator support metric measurements?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Yes, the Rafter Calculator provides full support for metric measurements including Centimeters (cm) and Meters (m), as well as Imperial Inches (in) and Feet-Inches (ft-in). Changing the unit selector automatically updates all input fields, labels, and output specifications.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 7 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">How do I use this Rafter Calculator?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>To use this Rafter Calculator, select your preferred measurement unit, choose whether you are entering Total Roof Span or Half Run, enter your horizontal distance, select your roof pitch, and specify your eave overhang. Use the advanced settings to fine-tune ridge board thickness, wall plate width, and rafter lumber depth.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 8 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">When should I use a Rafter Calculator?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>You should use a Rafter Calculator during the planning, estimation, and cutting phases of any roof framing project. It is ideal for estimating lumber quantities, preparing job-site cut lists, verifying birdsmouth notch depths, and checking slope angles for sheds, garages, home additions, and residential roofs.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 9 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">What is the difference between "Span" and "Run"?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Total Roof Span is the horizontal distance between the outside edges of opposite exterior wall plates. Roof Run is the horizontal distance a single rafter covers from the wall plate to the center of the building, equal to half the total span minus half the thickness of the ridge board.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 10 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">How do you calculate the standard length of a common rafter?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>The standard length of a common rafter from the ridge plumb cut line to the wall plate plumb cut line is calculated using Rafter Length = √(Run² + Rise²), where Run is half span minus half ridge thickness, and Rise is Run multiplied by the pitch ratio. Eave overhang is added separately.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 11 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">What does a "6/12 roof pitch" mean?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>A 6/12 roof pitch means the roof rises 6 units vertically for every 12 units of horizontal run. This represents a 50% slope grade with a pitch angle of 26.57 degrees, which is one of the most common standard roof slopes in residential construction.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 12 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">How does the ridge board affect the rafter calculation?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>The ridge board runs horizontally along the peak where opposing rafters meet. Because rafters abut the ridge board rather than each other, half of the actual ridge board thickness (e.g., 0.75 inches for a nominal 1.5-inch 2x ridge board) must be subtracted from the total building run before calculating rafter length.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ Item 13 -->
      <details class="faq-details">
        <summary class="faq-summary">Is there a Rafter Calculator app available for free download?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>This Rafter Calculator runs directly in your web browser as a responsive progressive web application, eliminating the need to download or install software from an app store. You can bookmark or save the page to your mobile home screen for instant offline-ready access on the job site.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

    </div>
  </section>

</div>

